justment": 0.0, "key_factors": ["Wisconsin's partisan lean is dead even and the presidential race has no incumbent", "No general-election polls or expert ratings have been published yet", "Primary-season turbulence (progressive/moderate split, election-denier GOP gubernatorial nominee) signals high volatility", "Mixed signals from neighboring Midwest primaries do not transfer cleanly to Wisconsin"], "rationale": "No general-election polls or expert ratings exist, and Wisconsin's fundamentals are evenly balanced with no incumbent on either side, so the race sits at an even margin with unusually wide uncertainty. The recent headlines mostly concern the governor's race rather than the presidential matchup.", "watch": "The first credible statewide polls or expert ratings, especially once both parties settle on nominees, could quickly break the tie in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin enters the 2028 presidential contest as close to a pure coin flip as any state offers. Its partisan lean is dead even, the incumbent president is term-limited, and no general-election polls or expert ratings have been published. With a balanced baseline and no measurable tilt toward either party, the race opens at dead center.\n\nState politics are unusually lively, with a contentious Democratic primary season, a Republican gubernatorial nominee criticized for election denial, and mixed progressive results in neighboring Michigan. But those storylines concern down-ballot contests and do not directly inform the presidential matchup. Until credible statewide polling arrives, the race is best treated as a genuine tossup with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.498, "margin": 0.0}, {"date": "2026-